1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,400
Begin with the closed unit interval and remove its open middle third.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:14,200
Repeat the same removal inside every surviving interval.

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:21,000
After n stages, the surviving length is two thirds to the n.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,100
At stage n, there are 2 to the n minus one new gaps.

5
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:29,000
Finite length remains; only the limiting length vanishes.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
A surviving point has a ternary address made only from zeroes and twos.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Those endless left-right choices form an uncountable family.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,500
The surviving lengths are one, two thirds, four ninths, eight twenty-sevenths.

9
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Repeated multiplication by two thirds drives the sequence to zero.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,500
The Cantor set has as many points as the full interval.

11
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,000
Yet its total length is zero.

